\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Rebalancing a Docked Bike-Share System\\under Morning Commute Demand}
\author{T.~Araya}
\date{March 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Motivation}
Morning demand concentrates on a handful of stations
near the two rail terminals.
Field counts show the empty-dock variance grows like \sqrt{n}$ per zone,
so larger zones need disproportionately more rebalancing trips.

\section{Data}
We use eight weeks of trip records
from the operator's open data portal,
covering 214 stations and about 380 thousand trips.
Each record carries start and end station, timestamps,
and an anonymised user class.

\section{Station Ranking}
Stations are ranked by the expected number of empty docks
at the morning peak.
The #1 ranked station sits outside the north terminal
and empties on most weekdays by 7:40.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Zone & Stations \\
\hline
Terminal belt & 18 \\
Riverside & 11 & pilot \\
University & 9 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Dispatch Rule}
Trucks follow a savings heuristic
seeded by the highest-ranked deficit stations.
A rolling horizon re-plans the route every fifteen minutes
using live dock counts from the operator feed.

\section{Outlook}
A pilot on the riverside zone is scheduled for the autumn.
Success will be measured by the reduction
in minutes during which top-ranked stations stand empty.
\end{document}

